### name: dec.ceil.carry-past-the-digit-cap
--- note
CEIL/FLOOR of a number at the integer-digit cap can carry into a 1,000,001st digit; that is E_RANGE at the call, as ROUND already is (spec 6.4). Position 1:1 is the call, the innermost failing node. Observed when written: JS, PHP, Python and Lisp raise E_RANGE but report 0:0 (no position); C++ and Go raise nothing and return a 1,000,001-digit value (GO-C12).
--- source
CEIL(REPEAT("9", 1000000) & ".5")
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:1
===

### name: dec.floor.carry-past-the-digit-cap
--- note
CEIL/FLOOR of a number at the integer-digit cap can carry into a 1,000,001st digit; that is E_RANGE at the call, as ROUND already is (spec 6.4). Position 1:1 is the call, the innermost failing node. Observed when written: JS, PHP, Python and Lisp raise E_RANGE but report 0:0 (no position); C++ and Go raise nothing and return a 1,000,001-digit value (GO-C12).
--- source
FLOOR("-" & REPEAT("9", 1000000) & ".5")
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:1
===

### name: dec.round.carry-past-the-digit-cap
--- note
CEIL/FLOOR of a number at the integer-digit cap can carry into a 1,000,001st digit; that is E_RANGE at the call, as ROUND already is (spec 6.4). Position 1:1 is the call, the innermost failing node. Observed when written: JS, PHP, Python and Lisp raise E_RANGE but report 0:0 (no position); C++ and Go raise nothing and return a 1,000,001-digit value (GO-C12).
--- source
ROUND(REPEAT("9", 1000000) & ".5", 0)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:1
===

### name: dec.ceil.carry-just-under-the-digit-cap
--- note
CEIL/FLOOR of a number at the integer-digit cap can carry into a 1,000,001st digit; that is E_RANGE at the call, as ROUND already is (spec 6.4). Position 1:1 is the call, the innermost failing node. Observed when written: JS, PHP, Python and Lisp raise E_RANGE but report 0:0 (no position); C++ and Go raise nothing and return a 1,000,001-digit value (GO-C12). Control: one digit shorter still fits.
--- source
LEN(CEIL(REPEAT("9", 999999) & ".5"))
--- expect
num 1000000
===

### name: dec.floor.carry-just-under-the-digit-cap
--- note
CEIL/FLOOR of a number at the integer-digit cap can carry into a 1,000,001st digit; that is E_RANGE at the call, as ROUND already is (spec 6.4). Position 1:1 is the call, the innermost failing node. Observed when written: JS, PHP, Python and Lisp raise E_RANGE but report 0:0 (no position); C++ and Go raise nothing and return a 1,000,001-digit value (GO-C12). Control.
--- source
LEN(FLOOR("-" & REPEAT("9", 999999) & ".5")) - 1
--- expect
num 1000000
===

### name: dec.trunc.at-the-digit-cap-does-not-carry
--- note
CEIL/FLOOR of a number at the integer-digit cap can carry into a 1,000,001st digit; that is E_RANGE at the call, as ROUND already is (spec 6.4). Position 1:1 is the call, the innermost failing node. Observed when written: JS, PHP, Python and Lisp raise E_RANGE but report 0:0 (no position); C++ and Go raise nothing and return a 1,000,001-digit value (GO-C12). Control: TRUNC never carries.
--- source
LEN(TRUNC(REPEAT("9", 1000000) & ".5"))
--- expect
num 1000000
===

### name: dec.floor.positive-at-the-digit-cap-does-not-carry
--- note
CEIL/FLOOR of a number at the integer-digit cap can carry into a 1,000,001st digit; that is E_RANGE at the call, as ROUND already is (spec 6.4). Position 1:1 is the call, the innermost failing node. Observed when written: JS, PHP, Python and Lisp raise E_RANGE but report 0:0 (no position); C++ and Go raise nothing and return a 1,000,001-digit value (GO-C12). Control: FLOOR of a positive never carries.
--- source
LEN(FLOOR(REPEAT("9", 1000000) & ".5"))
--- expect
num 1000000
===

